<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="D-sorbitol degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4101" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Of the ten existing hexitols (|FRAME: CPD-15703|, |FRAME: CPD-12811|, |FRAME: CPD-15702|, |FRAME: SORBITOL|, |FRAME: CPD-12810|, |FRAME: MANNITOL|, |FRAME: CPD-15701|, |FRAME: CPD-357|, |FRAME: CPD-369| and |FRAME: GALACTITOL|) only three occur naturally - |FRAME: MANNITOL|, |FRAME: SORBITOL| (also known as D-glucitol), and |FRAME: GALACTITOL| (owing to symmetry, D- and L-galactitol are identical). Each of these can be utilized by bacteria as a total source of carbon and energy. Sorbitol is one of several acyclic polyols or sugar alcohols found in higher plants |CITS:[BIELESKI82]|. In Rosaceae plants, sorbitol is the main photoassimilate produced in the leaves from which it is translocated to sink tissues |CITS:[WEBB62][BIELESKI85]|. Sorbitol synthesis shares a common hexose phosphate pool with sucrose synthesis, and it was recently shown in apple that synthesis pathways are not independent from one another |CITS:[ZHOU02]| as |FRAME:MONOMER-11709| was shown to be inhibited by |FRAME:D-SORBITOL-6-P|, an intermediate in sorbitol biosynthesis. D-sorbitol degradation is common in prokaryotes. For example, |FRAME: TAX-1423| can utilize it as a sole carbon source via the &lt;i&gt;gut&lt;/i&gt; operon (glucitol-utilizing transporter), consisting of a transporter and an NAD&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;-dependent glucitol dehydrogenase |CITS: [6402486][12897001]|. The pathway also occurs in plants, as translocated sorbitol is &apos;unloaded&apos; in fruit tissues and eventually converted to fructose. In plants that use sorbitol as the main product of photosynthesis D-sorbitol dehydrogenase is a key enzyme, supplying carbon during fruit development.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
